    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d539">

                <pb facs="0573" n="569" sameAs="frus:frus1939v03/pg569"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">123G861/786: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Japan</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Dooman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extract]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-08-13T15:00:00+09:00">August 13, 1939—3
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received August 13—8:37 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>405.…</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p>3. The first reaction here to the notice of termination of the commercial treaty
                    was strong but I did not believe that it would necessarily be lasting. That
                    belief is confirmed by observation during the past 2 weeks. As stated in one of
                    my recent despatches<anchor n="75" xml:id="d539-f1"/> the attitude <pb
                        facs="0574" n="570" xml:id="pg570"/> taken by our Government during the past
                    2 years has operated to give the Japanese the impression that the United States
                    (unlike Great Britain) relied on no one but itself to protect its rights and
                    interests in China and that it might some day move in that direction. And in
                    this sense the Japanese had been prepared for the shock even though anything so
                    drastic as the termination of the treaty was unexpected. Their present thought
                    seems to be to do nothing while watching carefully for further indications of
                    future American attitude. American stock is still higher than any other. The
                    next few months may bring developments of the most critical character and the
                    presence here of Mr. Grew, with the prestige which he commands in Japan as well
                    as in the United States, would be an incalculable service to our national
                        interests.<anchor n="76" xml:id="d539-f2"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dooman</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d539-f1" n="75" xml:id="d539-f1-content">
                <p>Despatch No. 4076, August 4, <ref target="#pg564">p. 564</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d539-f2" n="76" xml:id="d539-f2-content">
                <p>The Department, in its telegram No. 252, August 15, 5 p.m., said it “shares the
                    views expressed in concluding sentence”. (123G861/791)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
